    And here’s what I noticed, because since 3:07 I’d started noticing
everything. The café had been six noises: the grinder, the steamer, a
stroller squeaking, two guys arguing about the Yankees’ bullpen, the
freelancer’s keyboard, the Jerome Avenue traffic. The second Manny’s
hand touched the case, those six noises quit being six and became one,
the way a crowd turns into a crowd when somebody starts a clap. A
window sliding into its track. The stroller baby, who’d been working up
to a scream, sighed and closed her eyes.
    Manny didn’t notice. Manny never notices.
    “You good?” he said, watching me watch him.
    “Long night.”
